# 10. Understanding `nslookup google.com`

Suppose you run:

```bash
nslookup google.com
```

and receive:

```
google.com → 192.178.134.102
google.com → 192.178.134.100
google.com → 192.178.134.113
google.com → 192.178.134.138
google.com → 192.178.134.139
google.com → 192.178.134.101
```

Why are there multiple IPs?

Because:

> **One domain name can have multiple A records.**
> 

An A record maps:

```
hostname → IPv4 address
```

So:

```
google.com
    ↓
IP1
IP2
IP3
IP4
IP5
IP6
```

This provides multiple network entry points.

---

# 11. Very important: 6 IPs does NOT mean 6 backend servers

Wrong mental model:

```
IP1 = Server 1
IP2 = Server 2
IP3 = Server 3
```

Not necessarily.

One public IP may lead to a huge infrastructure behind it:

```
192.178.134.102
        ↓
Google frontend infrastructure
        ↓
Load balancing
        ↓
Thousands of internal servers
```

So:

> **An IP address is an entry point, not necessarily one physical computer.**
> 

This is an extremely important system-design concept.

---

# 12. DNS and Load Balancing can work together

A large architecture can conceptually look like:

```
                    google.com
                        ↓
                       DNS
                        ↓
              Multiple Public IPs
             /         |         \
            ↓          ↓          ↓
        Frontend    Frontend    Frontend
            \          |          /
             \         |         /
              Load balancing
                    ↓
             Backend fleet
```

There can therefore be multiple levels of distribution.

### Layer 1

DNS gives you one or more public entry points.

### Layer 2

The infrastructure behind that IP determines which actual machine/service handles the request.

